## How Polymarket.tips Surfaces Risk-Adjusted Conviction

Raw position size tells you nothing without context. A one million dollar position from a trader with ten million in verified profits is a 10 percent allocation — significant but measured. The same position from a trader with two million total represents 50 percent concentration and much higher effective leverage. The [convergence signal](/what-is-a-convergence-signal/) methodology at polymarket.tips accounts for this by weighting signals based on both absolute size and portfolio concentration. When multiple top traders independently take similar positions with measured allocation sizes, that signal carries more weight than a single whale making a concentrated bet. This distinction matters enormously for anyone attempting to follow smart money: you want to identify high-conviction, properly-sized positions, not desperate gamblers swinging for the fences.

Browsing the [live positions on Polymarket](https://polymarket.com/?r=POLYTips) shows you what traders hold, but understanding their effective leverage requires tracking their full portfolio context — exactly what the polymarket.tips leaderboard provides.

## Practical Leverage Risk Rules for Your Own Trading

The December Fed market offers a concrete example. With prices near 77 cents for a rate increase, the asymmetric payout means maximum loss is 77 cents per share while maximum gain is 23 cents. A trader comfortable risking 5 percent of their portfolio on this outcome would size their position so that a complete loss equals that 5 percent threshold. If the position moves against them to 50 cents, they have lost roughly 35 percent of that position but only 1.75 percent of total portfolio — painful but survivable, with capital remaining to either average down or redeploy elsewhere. This framework scales to any market on [Polymarket](https://polymarket.com/?r=POLYTips). The formula is simple: determine your maximum acceptable loss in portfolio percentage terms, then size your position so that worst-case resolution equals that amount. Most blowups happen because traders reverse this logic, sizing based on potential gain rather than acceptable loss.

